Done. The fix is in `lib/core/request.js:495-502`:

```js
} else if (headerName === 'content-length') {
    if (request.contentLength !== null) {
      throw new InvalidArgumentError('duplicate content-length header')
    }
    // Only digit-only values are valid, e.g. no sign, no fraction and no
    // trailing garbage which would otherwise be silently dropped by parseInt.
    if (typeof val !== 'string' || !isDigitOnly(val)) {
      throw new InvalidArgumentError('invalid content-length header')
    }
    request.contentLength = parseInt(val, 10)
    ...
```

with a small helper near the top of the file (`lib/core/request.js:28-33`):

```js
// Verifies that a given value consists of one or more decimal digits only
const digitOnlyRegex = /^\d+$/

function isDigitOnly (value) {
  return value.length !== 0 && digitOnlyRegex.test(value)
}
```

Behavior:
- Rejected with `InvalidArgumentError('invalid content-length header')`: `''`, `' '`, `'10.5'`, `'+10'`, `'-1'`, `'10abc'`, `' 10'`, `'10 '`, `'0x10'`, arrays (non-string), and numeric values that stringify to non-digit forms (`10.5`, `-1`, `NaN`).
- Unchanged: decimal integer strings and integer numbers (e.g. `Buffer.byteLength(body)`), which are coerced to a digit-only string earlier in `processHeader`.

Tests: added `request malformed content-length header` and `request valid content-length header` to `test/content-length.js`. Ran `test/content-length.js` (12 pass), plus `test/invalid-headers.js`, `test/no-strict-content-length.js`, `test/client.js`, `test/fetch/client-fetch.js`, `test/client-request.js`, `test/client-post.js`, `test/headers-as-array.js`, `test/request-crlf.js`, `test/request-timeout.js` — all pass. I could not run eslint; that command needed approval I didn't have.

